Subject: VMS: read input that is not a terminal with read()

vms_read() and RealWaitForChar() always use terminal-driver QIOs on a
channel assigned to SYS$OUTPUT. When the input is a file or a mailbox
(DEFINE/USER SYS$INPUT, PIPE, a batch job or a command procedure) and so
SYS$OUTPUT is usually not a terminal either, the QIOs fail, vms_read()
returns no characters and RealWaitForChar() reports nothing to read, so
Vim loops for ever using all the CPU: "vim -es <script" never ends.

When read_cmd_fd is not a terminal, read it with read() as os_unix.c does
(the C RTL returns one record per call, with its newline), and report it
as always ready. The end of the input then ends Vim as on Unix.

--- a/src/os_vms.c
+++ b/src/os_vms.c
@@ -347,6 +347,16 @@
     ITEM	itmlst[2];     // terminates on everything
     static long trm_mask[8] = {-1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1};
 
+    // Input from a file or a mailbox (DEFINE SYS$INPUT, PIPE, a batch job or a
+    // command procedure) cannot be read with the terminal driver's QIOs: read
+    // it with read(), as on Unix, so that "vim -es <script" works and the end
+    // of the input is seen.
+    if (!isatty(read_cmd_fd))
+    {
+	len = read(read_cmd_fd, inbuf, nbytes);
+	return len < 0 ? 0 : len;
+    }
+
     // whatever happened earlier we need an iochan here
     if (!iochan)
 	tt_mode = get_tty();
@@ -849,6 +859,10 @@
     unsigned int convert_operation = LIB$K_DELTA_SECONDS_F;
     float sec =(float) msec/1000;
 
+    // Input that is not a terminal is always ready; vms_read() reads it.
+    if (!isatty(read_cmd_fd))
+	return 1;
+
     // make sure the iochan is set
     if (!iochan)
 	get_tty();
